"""English lessons with brief Roman Hinglish examples for the eleven-slide deck."""
PAGES = []

def page(slide, title, *blocks, refs=()):
    PAGES.append(dict(slide=slide,title=title,blocks=list(blocks),refs=list(refs)))

page(1, 'The problem SAFE-Pipe tries to solve',
('Start with a familiar job', 'Imagine a company calculating total sales for each city. The computer must read orders, group them and calculate totals. How many worker processes should it use? How many threads should each worker have? How much memory should each worker be allowed? Too little can slow the job or contribute to failure; larger allocations increase the configured resource burden. This sales example is illustrative, not our measured dataset.'),
('What we built', 'SAFE-Pipe is a configuration advisor. For a supported workload and a deadline, it estimates runtime under the available settings, applies constraints and recommends a setting. If none qualifies, it abstains and explains why. The current advise command performs inference; it does not launch the full Dask job.'),
('Unpack the project title', '<b>Runtime</b> is execution time. <b>Cost</b> here is a configured thread/memory-time proxy, not rupees or an AWS bill. <b>SLA constraints</b> mean a declared time limit must be respected by the eligibility checks. <b>Failure-aware</b> means unsupported cases, warning evidence and failed outcomes are not ignored; it does not mean a trained failure-probability model exists.'),
('Why the slide asks about a sample', 'The small-sample question belongs to the later research direction. After building the original runtime advisor, we asked whether a short trial could reveal memory difficulties before a full job. In the tested short workloads, probes added cost without demonstrating better choices than stronger metadata. That is a useful finding, even though it did not justify enabling probing.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Job chalne se pehle hum setting suggest karte hain. Baad mein check kiya: chhota trial extra useful information deta hai, ya bas extra time leta hai?"'), refs=('M1','M4','R7'))

page(1, 'Job, task, partition, worker and thread',
('Pipeline and job', 'A <b>pipeline</b> is a sequence such as read -> clean -> join -> aggregate -> output. A <b>job</b> is one requested computation being executed. Our benchmarks are controlled, operator-focused jobs. We have not evaluated an entire production ETL platform.'),
('Partition and task', 'A <b>partition</b> is a chunk of data. A <b>task</b> is a unit of work in an execution graph: reading a chunk, computing a partial sum or performing part of a shuffle. One job can contain many tasks. Therefore, 96 benchmark runs do not mean 96 Dask tasks; each run executes its own graph.'),
('Scheduler, worker and thread', 'The <b>scheduler</b> assigns ready tasks to workers. A <b>worker</b> is a process that executes them. A <b>thread</b> is an execution lane inside that process. Two workers with two threads each give four configured lanes. This does not guarantee full utilization of four CPU cores at every instant.'),
('Why this is still a single-machine study', 'Dask supports distributed execution, but our LocalCluster used multiple processes on one Windows PC. We measured scheduling, data exchange and worker limits on that host, not performance across several machines. The host had about 31.4 GiB of physical RAM; the deliberately smaller worker caps remained 1 GB and 2 GB.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Restaurant ka poora order job hai; chopping aur boiling tasks hain; ingredient trays partitions hain; manager scheduler hai; cooks workers hain. Ek order mein bahut tasks ho sakte hain."'), refs=('M1','M2'))

page(2, 'The journey from the starting point to today',
('1. Establish a controlled experiment', 'The repository acquired workload definitions, schemas, the eight-setting configuration grid, record storage and a planner. Windows Dask setup and small pilots were checked. Four synthetic datasets and their deadlines were then frozen: their identities and settings were recorded so comparisons would not silently use different inputs.'),
('2. Complete the original midterm study', 'Four profiles x eight configurations x three repeats produced 96 physical runs. All succeeded and met their engine deadlines. An audited summary table supplied the grouped training, calibration and test splits for the runtime model. Millions of input records became run summaries; they were not millions of model-training examples.'),
('3. Improve the advisor policy', 'Balanced, fastest and resource modes were added, along with exact-profile support, artifact identity checks, per-candidate explanations and abstention. These policy changes reused the fitted runtime model. Changing the selection preference does not necessarily require retraining the predictor.'),
('4. Investigate harder research questions', 'The separate research branch screened larger and uneven inputs. Stage A studied full-job memory pressure; Stage B compared matching small samples. Worker telemetry and one-probe economics were then reviewed. These records are separate from the original 96-row model table.'),
('5. Reach the current checkpoint', 'The original-advisor validation software passed 143 Windows tests, but the fresh 96-run study has not started. The research decision code passed 38 tests, while automatic probe admission remains disabled. Software readiness and demonstrated experimental benefit are separate milestones.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Pehle advisor banaya; phir uski limitations investigate ki. Ab fresh validation baaki hai. Code ready hona aur naya experiment complete hona alag cheezein hain."'), refs=('M1','M7','R1','R3','R4','R8'))

page(2, 'Git branches and the earlier 6 GB question',
('What a branch means', 'Git stores a versioned history. A commit is a saved checkpoint; a branch is a parallel line of development. Branches help the team keep exploratory changes separate from stable work. Pushing a branch shares its commits; it does not automatically merge them into main or execute experiments.'),
('The three relevant branches', '<b>main</b> contains the merged original advisor/study work. <b>codex/partition-probe-research</b> holds separate probe experiments, memory exports and the guarded research prototype. <b>codex/main-advisor-completion</b> holds fresh-validation infrastructure and the current documentation work. Discussing research results in a report does not integrate that code into the original advisor.'),
('What approximately 6.78 GiB referred to', 'The retained historical note refers to prepared Parquet and sample storage across two screen directories. It was not one training database, the saved LightGBM model, or the size of 68 text log entries. Multiple full inputs and sample variants occupied disk space. This is a historical storage explanation, not a current disk inventory.'),
('Keep four kinds of storage separate', 'Parquet files contain compressed input data on disk. RAM holds decoded data, intermediates and buffers during execution. runs.csv contains a compact summary for each run. The model artifact stores learned trees. These are different objects with different sizes. The guide uses pinned original evidence at caa8a8a, research evidence at edef1cd and the checked status at 8179c07.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Data ki files, run ka log aur trained model teen alag cheezein hain. 6 GB prepared files hone ka matlab 6 GB ka LightGBM model train hua nahi hai."'), refs=('M1','M10','R1'))

page(3, 'Architecture: learning once, advising per request',
('The upper row: offline fitting', 'Create and identify the fixed inputs; execute the configuration matrix; audit the records; split the family/configuration groups; fit the model; calibrate the upper multiplier; save the model and its support bindings. This preparation is not repeated every time someone requests advice.'),
('The lower row: request-time advice', 'Read the declared workload descriptors, SLA and mode. Check support. Construct eight candidate feature rows for the same workload. Use the saved model to predict eight runtimes and upper estimates. Apply deadline/resource constraints, score the eligible choices and return a winner or an abstention.'),
('What bindings protect', 'A model was fitted against particular feature definitions, configurations and data versions. Bindings use hashes and identities to check that relationship. Reusing a configuration ID after silently changing its memory limit would invalidate that relationship. A hash identifies bytes; it does not by itself prove an experiment is scientifically correct.'),
('Training versus inference', 'Training learns from features paired with measured runtimes. Inference uses the saved model to make predictions. The target job does not need to execute before a runtime prediction is produced. Its actual runtime becomes available only after execution.'),
('Read the two status strips separately', 'The fresh Windows validation protocol is implemented and software-verified. The research prototype has automatic probing disabled. These statements concern different paths; neither says that a new full validation study or a physical one-probe controller has already run.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Offline phase mein model ko purane examples se sikhaya. Request aane par wahi saved model estimate deta hai; har request par training dobara nahi hoti."'), refs=('M3','M4','M7','R7'))

page(3, 'Exactly what information LightGBM receives',
('Six workload fields', '<b>family</b>: the workload label. <b>input_bytes</b>: compressed input size. <b>estimated_rows</b>: frozen row descriptor. <b>file_count</b>: declared files. <b>partition_count</b>: declared input partitions. <b>skew_score</b>: a fixed family tag in this version, not a computed statistical measurement of skew.'),
('Six configuration fields', '<b>config_id</b>: candidate identity. <b>n_workers</b>: worker processes. <b>threads_per_worker</b>: configured threads in each process. <b>memory_limit_gb</b>: the frozen nominal encoding. <b>allocated_vcpus</b>: workers x threads, despite its historical name. <b>allocated_gb</b>: workers x nominal per-worker cap. These twelve columns form the explicit pre-run whitelist.'),
('How candidates become different inputs', 'For one sort_merge request, the workload descriptors remain fixed. Moving from w2_t2_1g to w2_t2_2g changes the configuration fields. family and config_id are converted into one-hot indicator columns. The model receives candidate-specific numeric feature rows, not the original Parquet contents.'),
('What it must not receive', 'The target run\'s actual engine_seconds, status, SLA miss or peak memory are post-run observations, not inputs. Supplying them beforehand would leak the answer. The SLA and preference mode affect advisor selection; they are not columns in the original twelve-field runtime model.'),
('Important descriptor limitations', 'Matching descriptors does not prove matching file contents or hardware. The original join row descriptor counts left rows; its declared file count omits the additional right-side file. These frozen feature semantics must not be silently changed under an already fitted model.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Same job ko eight settings ke saath socho. Job ki details same, setting ki details change. Model har setting ka time predict karta hai; actual result pehle se nahi dekhta."'), refs=('M2','M3','M4'))

page(3, 'Why investigate execution-based profiling?',
('Profiling simply means collecting useful characteristics', '<b>Metadata profiling</b> reads information such as input size, row counts, schema and file layout. <b>Execution profiling</b> runs a smaller version of the computation and observes its time or memory behavior. Both collect information, but they have different costs and reveal different things.'),
('What the original profiler actually knew', 'The original advisor relied heavily on frozen manifests and family tags. It was not a universal profiler that deeply inspected every arbitrary input. For example, skew_score was a declared family tag, not an automatically measured frequency distribution. Those descriptors supported the bounded original runtime study.'),
('The motivation for a real sample run', 'Equal compressed sizes need not imply equal peak RAM. Joins, shuffles, concentrated keys and uneven partitions can create different working sets. The research hypothesis was that a small executed sample might reveal difficulty that metadata alone missed. This was a question to test, not an assumed advantage.'),
('What the investigation found', 'The binary warning signal was quiet for every Stage B probe. Continuous peak/cap readings later showed variation, but they separated the same cases that stronger metadata already identified. Extra measurement did not demonstrate better configuration choices in these cases. Therefore, replacing the metadata choice was not justified.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Metadata ko galat maan kar replace nahi kiya. Humne poocha: sample run ki extra information, uske extra time ke worth hai kya? Current short jobs mein benefit prove nahi hua."'), refs=('M2','R4','R6','R7'))

page(4, 'The four workloads: what the code does',
('1. scan_filter', 'Read the files, retain rows where value > 0.5, and compute the mean of the selected values. An illustrative use case is averaging readings above a threshold. The generated flag column is not used by this filter.'),
('2. groupby', 'Group records by key, sum value within each key, and take the mean of those sums. City-wise sales totals are a useful analogy. Combining records with the same key can require work across partitions.'),
('3. skewed_join', 'Perform an inner join of left and right tables on key, then sum the joined payload. Skew means some keys occur much more frequently than others. The original join and the later research join use different generation recipes; their sizes and structures must not be mixed.'),
('4. The sort_merge label', 'The repository uses set_index(key, shuffle="tasks"), then extracts the first payload value in each resulting partition and sums those values. This forces a key-based shuffle/index operation. Despite its name, this implementation is not a second-table merge-sort benchmark.'),
('One input or several?', 'The original study contains four separately generated frozen profiles, one per family. Each profile was reused across eight configurations. Later research introduced additional instances and samples. Repeating one fixed input with different worker settings does not create a new dataset each time.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Ek hi sales table ko eight settings par chalana eight datasets nahi. Hamare original study mein four alag profiles the; har profile ko eight settings par repeat kiya."'), refs=('M2','M5'))

page(4, 'Input sizes, eight configurations and deadlines',
('Read the slide table carefully', 'scan_filter: 23.32 million declared rows, 198.45 MB compressed input, 2.6 s SLA. groupby: 14.97 million, 198.49 MB, 7.9 s. skewed_join: 17.04 million left rows, 174.60 MB across both inputs, 4.3 s. sort_merge: 12.77 million, 178.38 MB, 13.1 s. These are decimal MB on disk, not RAM peaks.'),
('The join has extra input', 'It also has 852,000 right-table rows. There are 16 left files plus one right file: 17 physical files. The frozen descriptors still record left-row count and 16 files. Explain this limitation rather than treating the descriptor as a complete physical inventory or silently changing the fitted inputs.'),
('How eight settings arise', 'Workers {1,2} x threads per worker {1,2} x memory per worker {1 GB,2 GB} = eight. IDs are w1_t1_1g, w1_t1_2g, w1_t2_1g, w1_t2_2g, w2_t1_1g, w2_t1_2g, w2_t2_1g and w2_t2_2g. For w2_t2_1g, totals are four configured threads and 2 GB of configured worker cap.'),
('Where the SLAs came from', 'Separate pilots used w1_t1_2g. For each family, three pilot engine times supplied a median; 1.5 times that median was rounded up to 0.1 seconds. These study deadlines were frozen before scored collection. They are not externally supplied customer contracts.'),
('Configured cap is not reserved RAM', 'Summing worker limits gives a configured allowance. Actual use can be lower or temporarily higher, and other processes also consume memory. When explaining the slide\'s "reserves more RAM" wording, distinguish allowance from physically reserved or measured memory.'),
('Hinglish example', '"w2_t2_1g mein 1 GB har worker ka hai. Do workers hain, isliye total cap 2 GB. Iska matlab poore laptop ka sirf 2 GB RAM use hoga nahi hai."'), refs=('M2','M5','M4'))

page(4, 'What the 96 runs and their timers mean',
('A group is not a run', 'One family/configuration pair is a group in the original split. Four families x eight settings give 32 groups. Each has three executions, producing 96 runs. Some planners include repeat identity in their use of "cell" and therefore count 96 planned cells. "96 runs across 32 profile/configuration groups" avoids ambiguity.'),
('Why repeat three times?', 'Background work, cache state and normal execution variation affect timings. Repeats provide an observed range and median. Repeating the same input does not create independent new workloads; testing broader scope requires distinct, untouched instances.'),
('Engine time is not total waiting time', 'The original engine timer starts after cluster/client creation and covers graph construction through the completed scalar result. It excludes input generation, profiling and cluster startup/teardown. Meeting a 2.6-second engine deadline therefore does not prove a user receives the complete response within 2.6 seconds of clicking.'),
('What 96/96 demonstrates', 'All scored original jobs succeeded and met their respective engine deadlines. There was no observed failure variation, and peak_memory_bytes was missing in the original table. This table cannot support a learned failure classifier or a measured peak-memory prediction result.'),
('A defensible presentation statement', '"All 96 runs met their engine deadlines within four fixed profiles on one Windows host. That is a bounded observation, not a universal safety guarantee." Inspect status, sla_miss and the missing-memory field definitions in the data dictionary.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Teen repeats se timing ka variation dikhta hai. Same input teen baar chalane se teen naye types ke workload test nahi ho jaate."'), refs=('M2','M6'))
page(5, 'LightGBM: what the model learns',
('The basic idea of boosted trees', 'A decision tree uses questions about features to produce a prediction. Boosting combines multiple trees that progressively address prediction errors. LightGBM implements gradient-boosted trees. We use regression, where the output is a continuous runtime, rather than classification into yes/no categories.'),
('What one training example represents', 'A benchmark summary row contains workload descriptors, configuration fields and a measured engine_seconds value. The model learns from these summary rows. Millions of records in the Parquet inputs do not become millions of model-training examples. Only 36 run-summary rows were used for the original fit.'),
('Why predict log-runtime?', 'The code fits log(engine_seconds) and applies exp() to obtain a positive point prediction in seconds. Log scale can be useful for representing relative differences. This is an engineering choice, not a transformation proved optimal by our study. The result is an estimate, not the actual future runtime or a guaranteed arithmetic mean.'),
('The fixed settings', 'The configuration specifies 120 trees, up to seven leaves, learning rate 0.05, minimum child samples three, L2 regularization one, random seed 2026 and one training thread. Learning rate controls update size; leaves control tree complexity; regularization discourages overly flexible fitting. These settings are not the documented winner of an archived hyperparameter search.'),
('Why this model?', 'Boosted trees were a practical option for structured tabular inputs. The recorded comparator is a family-median baseline. We did not demonstrate superiority over XGBoost, neural networks or every other learner. The later probe studies did not retrain this runtime model.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Model ke examples business table ki individual rows nahi hain. Example hai: is workload ko is setting par chalaya, aur itna time laga."'), refs=('M3','M8','E2'))

page(5, 'Training, calibration and testing have different roles',
('Training learns the predictor', 'Twelve family/configuration groups x three repeats give 36 fitting rows. The model learns relationships between their features and measured runtimes. Other configuration groups from the same workload families can appear in calibration and testing.'),
('Calibration sets the margin', 'A separate twelve groups / 36 rows determine the upper multiplier from log-prediction errors. The maximum residual within each group is used; negative residuals are not discarded beforehand. Calibration does not refit the trees. It adds an empirically determined margin to the point prediction.'),
('Testing evaluates the frozen choices', 'The remaining eight groups / 24 rows supply the reported error and coverage. They must not be used to fit the predictor or choose its calibration margin. The split is 36 + 36 + 24 = 96, not a standard 80/20 split.'),
('Why keep repeats together?', 'Suppose groupby with w2_t1_1g is executed three times. Putting one repeat in training and another in testing would place almost the same situation on both sides. Grouping keeps all three executions of that family/configuration pair in one split and prevents that form of repeat leakage.'),
('What this does not solve', 'All splits still concern the same four frozen profiles. This is not a test on unseen workload families, sizes or machines. Grouping also does not establish exchangeability: the statistical requirement that calibration and future examples behave comparably. A distribution change can undermine the coverage interpretation.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Training prediction sikhati hai; calibration margin set karti hai; test final check hai. Same setting ke repeats ko alag splits mein daalne se test unnecessarily easy ho sakta hai."'), refs=('M3','M8','M9'))

page(5, 'Understanding 13.84% MdAPE',
('Start with one prediction error', 'Absolute percentage error = abs(predicted - actual) / actual x 100. For an illustrative actual time of 10 s, either a 12 s prediction or an 8 s prediction has 20% absolute error. The absolute value removes direction. These numbers are teaching examples, not project measurements.'),
('Then take the median', 'Calculate each test row\'s percentage error, sort the values and take the middle. With an even number, average the two central values. Illustrative errors of 5%, 10%, 20%, 30% and 100% have a median of 20%. The median is less sensitive to extremes than the mean, but does not describe the worst case.'),
('Our recorded result', 'Across 24 held-out rows, LightGBM MdAPE was 13.842297%, shown as 13.84%. The baseline was 23.013084%, shown as 23.01%. The difference is approximately 9.17 percentage points. It describes this test set, not every dataset or future execution.'),
('What the baseline does', 'For each family, take the median runtime in the training split. Predict that same value for test rows from that family. It does not model configuration effects in detail. LightGBM can use candidate fields and achieved lower typical absolute relative error in this comparison.'),
('Reading the two graphs', 'In the error chart, a shorter bar is better. In the scatter plot, x is actual runtime and y is predicted runtime. The dashed y=x line indicates exact agreement; above it is overprediction and below it is underprediction. Repeated points are not independent new workloads. Do not call 100 minus MdAPE "accuracy".'),
('Hinglish example', '"13.84% error ka matlab 86.16% classification accuracy nahi. Hum seconds predict kar rahe hain, correct/incorrect labels count nahi kar rahe."'), refs=('M3','M9'))

page(5, 'Where the 1.688 runtime multiplier comes from',
('Point estimate versus upper estimate', 'The model produces a point estimate t. The saved upper estimate is U = t x exp(q), with q approximately 0.523642. Therefore U is approximately 1.688 x t. An illustrative t=2 s becomes U=3.376 s. This is a calibrated margin, not a guarantee.'),
('The calculation step by step', 'For each calibration row, calculate log(actual runtime) minus the model\'s log prediction. Take the largest residual among each group\'s three repeats: 36 rows become twelve group scores. With alpha=0.1, the finite-sample rank is ceil((12+1) x 0.9)=12. The largest of those twelve scores determines the multiplier.'),
('What 24/24 covered means', 'Every held-out runtime was at or below its upper estimate: 24/24 rows and 8/8 groups. This is a coverage result, not exact point-prediction accuracy. A wider upper margin can improve observed coverage while making it harder for a candidate to meet a tight deadline.'),
('Why it is not a universal promise', 'The test contains only eight groups from four profiles on one host. New layouts, software, hardware or workloads can change behavior. Keeping repeats together does not establish a comparable future distribution. The result does not prove universal 90% completion for future selected recommendations.'),
('How the advisor uses it', 'The upper estimate is checked against the deadline; the point estimate is used for scoring. The same multiplier applies to all candidates. It does not produce separate failure probabilities for them. "Eligible with this margin" is more accurate than "guaranteed safe".'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Point estimate expected time ka andaaza hai; upper estimate uske upar margin lagata hai. Test mein sab cover hue, lekin har future job ke liye guarantee nahi bani."'), refs=('M3','M4','M8','M9'))

page(6, 'Read the balanced objective in plain English',
('Eligibility comes before ranking', 'The advisor first checks support, deadline and resource budgets. An ineligible option cannot win merely because its score is attractive. The remaining options are ranked by J. An objective expresses how the decision values time and resources.'),
('The symbols', '<b>t</b>: predicted engine seconds. <b>D</b>: requested engine deadline. <b>P</b>: workers x threads per worker. <b>M</b>: total configured worker cap. <b>lambda</b>: thread share within the resource term. <b>beta</b>: latency share in the overall score. Defaults lambda=0.5 and balanced beta=0.5 are declared preferences, not trained coefficients.'),
('Q measures normalized allocation', 'Q = lambda x P/4 + (1-lambda) x M/4 GB. The grid\'s reference values are four threads and four decimal GB. Dividing by them makes the components dimensionless. These references stay fixed even if a budget removes some alternatives; another candidate\'s score does not change simply because an option disappeared.'),
('R combines time and resources; J expresses the preference', 'R = (t/D) x Q. Then J = beta x (t/D) + (1-beta) x R. Balanced mode weighs both terms. Fastest mode uses beta=1 and minimizes t/D. Resource mode uses beta=0 and minimizes R, not memory allocation alone.'),
('What the score is not', 'J does not measure rupees, electricity, actual CPU utilization or peak RAM. The 50/50 weights were not proved universally optimal. The formula makes a preference explicit; independent experiments must assess its outcome benefit.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Pehle dekho setting allowed hai ya nahi. Phir allowed options mein time aur resources ka balance compare karo. Sabse fast option zaroori nahi balanced winner ho."'), refs=('M4',))

page(6, 'Worked example: why 2 GB total wins',
('Candidate A: the balanced winner', 'w2_t2_1g has two workers, two threads each and 1 GB per worker: P=4 and M=2 GB. Its point estimate is about 2.29 s, with D=13.1 s. Using the rounded slide values, Q=0.5 x 1 + 0.5 x 0.5 = 0.75. Then t/D is about 0.1748, R about 0.1311, and J about 0.1530.'),
('Candidate B: the fastest winner', 'w2_t2_2g still has four threads but M=4 GB. Its point estimate is about 2.15 s. Q=1; t/D, R and J are each approximately 0.1641. It is slightly faster in prediction, but has twice the total configured memory. Candidate A has the smaller balanced score. These calculations use rounded values.'),
('The actual trade-off', 'The point predictions differ by about 0.14 seconds. Under the declared balanced preference, that small predicted speed advantage does not outweigh the larger allocation. This is not a newly measured 0.14-second saving. Fastest mode values only time after eligibility, so it chooses B.'),
('Resource mode and units', 'The saved resource-mode example chooses w1_t2_1g: one worker, two threads, 1 GB total. This slide compares 2 GB and 4 GB <b>total</b>. Stage A later compares 1 GB and 2 GB <b>per worker</b>. Confusing these scopes changes the meaning of the result.'),
('How to explain it aloud', '"We compare predicted speed and configured resource-time among eligible settings. In this sort example, the lower total memory cap gives the lower balanced score, even though another setting has a slightly lower predicted runtime."'),
('Hinglish example', '"Thoda sa faster hone ke liye memory cap double karni pad rahi thi. Hamari balanced preference ne smaller cap ko better trade-off maana."'), refs=('M4','M11'))

page(6, 'Reading eligibility, upper ticks and abstention',
('What the horizontal chart shows', 'Each row is one configuration. The dot is its point prediction t; the end tick is its upper estimate U. The orange vertical line is the 13.1-second deadline. The segment is specifically point-to-upper, not a generic two-sided confidence interval. The highlighted row is the balanced winner.'),
('Why there are no rejected red candidates here', 'All eight candidates have upper estimates within 13.1 seconds and pass the resource checks. Selection is therefore decided by score after eligibility. Saying that red candidates were rejected would not explain this particular example.'),
('The checks', 'The workload descriptors must match the supported profiles; artifact bindings must hold; U must meet the deadline; optional total-thread/total-memory budgets must pass; and the coarse input-byte guard must pass. A deadline reserve can ask for extra headroom. A guard based on compressed bytes is not proof of per-worker peak-memory safety.'),
('What happens at a 0.01-second SLA?', 'No upper estimate satisfies such a tight deadline, so the advisor abstains. This is an intentional result with a reason, not a crash. Unknown profiles can also trigger abstention. Forcing an unsupported input into the nearest known category would overstate the evidence.'),
('Advice does not prove execution', 'The saved JSON records recommendations and comparisons. It does not show that the chosen setting has just executed the exact full input. Prospective validation and later advice-to-execution binding address that gap. An evidence viewer is not a completed product interface.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Abstain ka matlab system fail nahi hua. Matlab: is deadline aur known settings ke andar responsible suggestion nahi mil raha."'), refs=('M4','M11'))

page(7, 'Four research methods, but only three sample probes',
('What a probe is', 'An execution probe runs the workload on a smaller sample before the full job, hoping to obtain useful time or memory information cheaply. Cheap is relative: when the full job takes only a few seconds, process startup and cleanup can make a small probe expensive.'),
('1. full: the reference', 'Execute the complete input at the declared target cap. This is the reference against which sample behavior is compared, not a small probe. The initial research table therefore contains four methods but only three sample-based probe methods.'),
('2. uniform: less data, the same cap', 'Select an exact random quarter of the fact rows while retaining the target worker cap. For an illustration, sample 25 of 100 fact rows. The smaller data has more memory headroom relative to its size, which can make full-input pressure disappear.'),
('3. uniform_memory: the same sample, a mapped cap', 'Use the same uniform sample files but reduce the cap using a fixed floor plus fraction scaling. Data is held constant while allowance changes. The aim is to avoid making the sample unrealistically easy; it is not a theorem that pressure will be preserved.'),
('4. partition_tail: emphasize larger input partitions', 'Keep the same total fact-row budget, preferentially sampling larger input partitions. Use the same memory mapping as uniform_memory. Large input files are not necessarily hot keys or costly post-shuffle partitions. The join dimension remains complete, so equal fact-row budgets do not imply equal total bytes.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Chaar methods ka matlab chaar chhote probes nahi: ek full reference aur teen sample probes. Uniform_memory mein sample wahi hai, memory cap change hoti hai."'), refs=('R1',))

page(7, 'The 768-attempt screen and what it taught us',
('How the matrix was formed', 'Two families x three sizes x two patterns give twelve instances. Each has eight configurations, two repeats and four methods: 12 x 8 x 2 x 4 = 768 attempts. This contains 192 full references and 576 probe executions. It is separate from the original 96-run model study.'),
('Observed outcomes', 'There were 759 successes, one timeout and eight execution/worker errors. A timeout is not a successful runtime. Failures must remain visible rather than being dropped to improve the comparison. Condition and attempt records were retained.'),
('The ranking question', 'If A is fastest on full data, does a small sample also rank A first? A change in order can lead the sample to choose a poor configuration. Ranking fidelity means preserving that order. Engine regret compares the full runtime of the sample-selected setting with the best full setting, as a relative percentage.'),
('The descriptive results', 'Across ten complete instances, median engine regret was 3.87% for uniform, 0.11% for uniform_memory and 8.17% for partition_tail. This is not a universal victory for memory scaling: some instances were incomplete/failed, mean and maximum regret differed, and selected historical full failures were retained. The tail heuristic did not automatically improve representation.'),
('Why move to Stage A?', 'The earlier uneven inputs could combine key skew with file imbalance. We needed to separate those factors and inspect pressure mechanisms. Sweeping many configurations also incurs preparation and lifecycle cost. Low engine regret alone cannot establish that an online selection policy saves total time.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Sample par A fast dikha, lekin full data par B fast nikla: ranking change hui. Aur A choose karne ke liye agar bahut probes chalaye, unka time bhi count karna padega."'), refs=('R1','R2'))

page(7, 'Stage A separates two kinds of imbalance',
('Key skew versus partition imbalance', '<b>Key skew</b> means unequal key frequencies: imagine 60 of 100 orders belonging to one customer. <b>Partition imbalance</b> means unequal input chunks: imagine files with 10, 10, 10 and 70 rows. Balanced files can still contain a dominant key; uneven files can contain balanced keys. These are teaching examples.'),
('The four patterns and actual recipe', 'Balanced has both knobs off; key-skew-only turns on key concentration; partition-imbalance-only turns on uneven files; both turns on both. The recipe uses seed 911, two million fact rows, eight fact files and sixteen payload columns. hot_fraction=0.25 and partition_imbalance=0.60 are generator parameters, not direct measured file-share percentages.'),
('The 48 full runs', 'Two families x four patterns give eight instances. Two configurations x three repeats give 48 attempts. Concurrency stays at two workers with one thread each. The comparison changes the cap from 1 GB to 2 GB per worker, reusing each instance\'s input.'),
('Read the heatmap', 'At 1 GB, groupby partition-imbalance-only succeeds 0/3; join partition-imbalance-only 2/3; join both 0/3. The remaining low-cap patterns succeed 3/3. Every 2 GB cell succeeds 3/3. Totals are 17/24 at 1 GB, 24/24 at 2 GB, and 41/48 overall.'),
('Do not smooth away the surprising case', 'Groupby both succeeds 3/3 even though partition-imbalance-only succeeds 0/3. The both case still has spill/pressure. This contradicts a simplistic "more skew always means worse" story. One seed and three repeats do not identify the exact causal mechanism.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Same key baar-baar aana aur ek file bahut badi hona alag problems hain. Successful job bhi pressure face kar sakti hai; success ka matlab quiet nahi."'), refs=('R3',))

page(7, 'Why spilling can occur with a 1 GB limit',
('RAM and disk serve different roles', 'Spilling moves some worker-held data from RAM to temporary disk storage. Input files already being on disk does not remove intermediate RAM needs. The cap is a Dask management target, not a hard operating-system reservation. Decoding, joins, shuffles and active tasks can increase transient memory.'),
('The default threshold map', 'The Dask documentation describes a managed-memory spill target near 60%, process-memory spilling near 70%, pausing new tasks near 80%, and nanny termination/restart near 95%. Thresholds are configurable. The retained termination analysis uses the recorded 0.95 threshold. [E1]'),
('What the nanny does', 'A nanny supervises a worker process. It may terminate and restart the worker after observing excessive memory. A job can recompute work and still finish. A nanny memory termination is not automatically an operating-system OOM kill. All seven Stage A errors had nanny memory-termination evidence.'),
('Why compressed bytes cannot predict the peak alone', 'Compressed files, decoded arrays, copies, buffers and working sets have different sizes. Layout and operators matter. "The file is 200 MB, therefore a 1 GB worker is always safe" is invalid. More host RAM does not automatically remove a local worker\'s pressure.'),
('Scope of the slide claim', 'The seven low-cap errors belong to Stage A\'s 48 attempts. The earlier screen\'s eight errors are a different count. The larger cap worked for these tested cases; that does not prove 2 GB is enough for every future input.'),
('Hinglish example', '"1 GB cap ek management target hai, sealed box nahi. Dask limit se pehle spill karke memory bachane ki koshish karta hai; sudden peak par worker restart bhi ho sakta hai."'), refs=('R3','R6','E1'))
page(8, 'Stage B: samples matched to full-job references',
('How Stage A and Stage B connect', 'Stage A supplied full-input outcomes for eight instances. Stage B examined smaller samples from those parent inputs. The question narrowed to whether a cheap sample warning revealed full-input pressure. Stage B did not repeat every sampling method from the earlier screen.'),
('One sampling approach, two fractions', 'The methods are uniform_memory_f0125 and uniform_memory_f0250: 1/8 and 1/4 of fact rows. Eight instances x two target caps x two fractions x three repeats = 96 probes. One deterministic sample per instance/fraction was reused across caps and repeats. The two fractions were not guaranteed to be nested samples.'),
('Calculate the effective cap', 'The formula is floor + fraction x (target cap - floor), rounded to bytes. The floor is 512 MiB = 536,870,912 bytes. At a 1 GB target and fraction 1/4, the effective cap is 536,870,912 + 0.25 x (1,000,000,000 - 536,870,912) = 652,653,184 bytes. At 1/8 it is 594,762,048 bytes. Ratios use this actual probe cap, not the full 1 GB target.'),
('Why include a floor?', 'The intention was to avoid shrinking all fixed overhead with the data fraction. But 512 MiB was a declared heuristic, not measured clean interpreter overhead. Fact rows, dimension memory and intermediates need not scale linearly. The entire right join dimension was retained.'),
('What quiet means', 'A successful run with complete clean diagnostics and no declared spill, pause, restart or termination warning. Missing information is not interpreted as zero or quiet. All 96 probes met this rule. That is an observation about the probes, not a certificate of full-job safety.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Quarter sample ka cap seedha 1 GB ka quarter nahi tha. Fixed floor bhi add kiya. Aur join ki right table full thi, isliye total working memory exactly quarter nahi hoti."'), refs=('R4','R5'))

page(8, 'What the eight discrepancies out of 32 mean',
('Understand the denominator first', 'Eight instances x two target caps x two fractions create 32 comparison groups, each with three probe repeats. A sample repeat and a full repeat from different sessions were not treated as synchronized pairs. The comparison uses group-level evidence.'),
('The eight marked cells', 'At the 1 GB target, four cases showed full pressure: groupby partition-imbalance-only, groupby both, join partition-imbalance-only and join both. Each was checked at two sample fractions: four x two = eight discrepancies. These are not eight independent workload discoveries.'),
('Pressure is different from failure', 'Groupby both succeeded in all three full runs, but showed pressure/spill. Join partition-only succeeded twice out of three, with pressure evidence. Therefore the slide says missed pressure, not eight failed full jobs. The ratio 8/32 is not a calibrated 25% future failure probability.'),
('Why the binary signal failed to discriminate', 'Every probe said quiet. A constant yes/no signal cannot distinguish easier and harder cases. Stage B\'s frozen time/resource rule chose the low target throughout. The selected historical full outcomes succeeded 17/24, compared with 24/24 for fixed 2 GB and the metadata reference. These are reused Stage A outcomes, not a fresh policy trial.'),
('What the result does not imply', 'It does not show that every sampling scheme or continuous memory signal is useless. It concerns the tested fractions, cap mapping, operators and one-seed inputs. It does show that sample success or no-warning alone was insufficient evidence of full-input quietness here.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Cross ka matlab sample quiet tha, full input pressured tha. Eight crosses = four cases ko two fractions par dekhna; eight independent failures nahi."'), refs=('R4','R5','R6'))

page(8, 'Understanding the 37 worker lifetimes',
('Jobs and lifetimes are different units', 'Stage A\'s 48 jobs plus Stage B\'s 96 give 144 jobs. Two initial workers per job give 288 initial lifetimes. The export retained 321 lifetimes: Stage A had 129 (96 initial and 33 added), and Stage B had 192 initial lifetimes. Added lifetimes were kept separate without inventing replacement pairings.'),
('What the number 37 counts', 'Thirty-seven distinct Stage A lifetimes had attributed nanny memory-termination events. This does not mean 37 failed jobs: Stage A had seven failed jobs. A job can lose more than one worker or recover and finish successfully. Added lifetimes and termination events are not the same counter.'),
('Read the axes and marks', 'The x-axis orders terminated lifetimes by their sampled peak ratio; it is not time. The y-axis is RSS divided by effective cap. The blue mark is the highest retained periodic sample; the green mark is the logged nanny-event RSS. The dashed line is 0.95. Their separation shows a difference between two observations.'),
('The actual findings', 'In 33/37 lifetimes, the retained sampled peak was below 0.95 of cap. Event RSS exceeded the saved peak in 36/37. All 37 event readings were above the threshold. Events followed the last retained sample by 0.016-0.419 s, median 0.165 s. Brief spikes or persistence gaps are consistent explanations; the exact contributions were not isolated.'),
('Why the distinction matters', 'A retained sample maximum is not the true continuous-time peak. Even the event reading is only an observation. Treating either as an exact uncensored memory-demand training label would overstate the measurement.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Speedometer ki photos mein max 80 dikha, lekin photos ke beech 100 ho sakta hai. Waise hi saved memory samples true maximum miss kar sakte hain."'), refs=('R6',))

page(8, 'Memory fields and the continuous signal',
('Recognize the different measurements', '<b>RSS</b> is resident process memory. <b>Managed memory</b> estimates Dask-tracked in-memory data. <b>Spill</b> records offloaded data. <b>Baseline RSS</b> is the first retained sample, not guaranteed clean idle memory. <b>Peak RSS</b> is the largest retained sample within its window. <b>Cap</b> is a setting, not observed consumption.'),
('What the export preserved', 'Run/cell identity, workload/configuration/fraction/repeat, effective cap, baseline and peak RSS, managed/spill peaks, sample counts/errors/windows, thresholds, status and restart/termination evidence. Missing values stayed missing. Restarted workers remained separate lifetimes. Exporting and checking existing plan/raw-record hashes did not rerun benchmarks.'),
('Look beyond the constant quiet flag', 'For one probe, q is the largest individual worker sampled-peak/cap ratio. Individual quarter-size, low-target runs ranged from 41.1-48.1% for historically quiet full cases and 59.1-67.6% for pressure-full cases. This is a development separation, not an independently measured classifier accuracy. Repeats share samples and workloads.'),
('Why the signal still did not justify probing', 'It separated the same four imbalanced-file cases that metadata already identified. A varying signal can exist without improving the decision. Paying for that signal produced no demonstrated additional configuration benefit in these short jobs.'),
('Two measurement traps', 'Do not add unsynchronized lifetime peaks and call the sum a concurrent cluster peak. Zero sampling errors also does not mean no spike was missed. Replacing the 512 MiB cap floor with the first sampled RSS would require a new justified measurement model.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Memory number mein difference mila, par recommendation wahi mili jo metadata se mil rahi thi. Extra information hona aur extra benefit hona same baat nahi."'), refs=('R6','R7'))

page(9, 'The cost gate: did the probe pay for itself?',
('What a gate means here', 'A gate is a condition for proceeding. We asked whether a probe adds useful decision information beyond metadata, and whether its possible benefit exceeds its full profiling cost. The current eight short-job instances did not demonstrate a convincing case for enabling it.'),
('What the bars represent', 'Median reconstructed request times were 4.79 s for size-aware metadata and the guarded default, 4.96 s for fixed 2 GB, 7.38 s for ambiguous-only probing, and 10.37 s for probing every request. These combine retained records under declared accounting rules; they are not newly measured online controller requests.'),
('Did the decisions change?', 'The stronger metadata and probe rules chose the same settings for all eight instances: four at 1 GB and four at 2 GB. The selected full outcomes were 24 unique historical references, all successful. Reusing those outcomes in multiple counterfactual decisions did not create 72 independent full runs.'),
('Two related timing numbers', '10.37 - 4.79 = 5.58 s is the difference between reported medians. Separately, median single-probe preparation plus lifecycle/preflight was 5.47 s. These need not be identical: the median of a sum generally differs from the sum of medians. Also, 4.79 s includes selected full execution; it is not metadata-read latency.'),
('Why probing remains off', 'The choices stayed the same while paid time increased. Keeping the gate closed follows the evidence. Longer jobs or repeated-job reuse could change the economics, but require their own fair baselines and fresh evaluation. This replay does not establish those benefits.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Agar bina test drive aur test drive ke baad same car choose hoti hai, toh test drive ka extra time justify karna padega. Yahan probe ne choices improve nahi ki."'), refs=('R7',))

page(9, 'What the one-probe prototype actually does',
('The stronger metadata calculation', 'The prototype uses fact/dimension rows, numeric payload count and the largest fact-file row count. Dense input estimate D = 8 x (payload columns+1) x fact rows + 16 x dimension rows. Largest fact partition L = 8 x (payload columns+1) x largest-file rows. This is layout arithmetic, not measured RSS or a fitted memory predictor.'),
('Lower and upper scenarios', 'With two workers, Mlo = 128 MiB + D/2 + L and Mhi = 128 MiB + D/2 + 3L. Choose 1 GB if Mhi <= 0.7 x 1 GB; otherwise 2 GB if Mhi <= 0.7 x 2 GB; otherwise abstain. These copy factors and reserves are hand-set development scenarios, not statistical confidence bounds.'),
('What the diagnostic probe would inspect', 'Use one quarter-size uniform sample, two single-thread workers and the 652,653,184-byte low-target cap. Check exactly one observation\'s identity and quality. For a clean observation, q < 0.55 suggests the lower cap; otherwise use the higher cap. The 0.55 cutoff was chosen after seeing development separation, not independently calibrated.'),
('What happens by default', 'admission_enabled=false. The default selects from metadata or abstains, without launching a physical probe. The counterfactual helper asks what one retained observation would imply. It cannot start Dask, generate data, retrain LightGBM or execute the draft pilot.'),
('Unknown or unsuitable evidence', 'Missing fields, wrong identity, warnings/restarts or an unsupported economic case cannot justify a lower-cap override. Diagnostic classification and permission to probe are separate. The proposed 48-attempt pilot is a disabled future draft, distinct from the completed Stage A 48 runs.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Rule ko old observation dekar check kar sakte hain, bina naya probe chalaye. Diagnostic answer milna physical experiment ki permission milna nahi hai."'), refs=('R7','R8','R9'))

page(9, 'Where execution profiling spends extra time',
('Break the timer into stages', 'Prepare/read/write the sample; verify identities in preflight; start a cluster and worker processes; initialize libraries; build and schedule the graph; execute the sample; retain telemetry; clean up. Together these can exceed a tiny computation\'s engine time. Full-input generation and research-wide integrity audits are setup costs, not automatically per-request profiling charges.'),
('From many probes to one', 'The screen compared multiple methods and settings. Stage B\'s diagnostic selection charged two caps x three repeats = six probes per fraction, plus preparation once. Median profiling cost alone was 24.62 s at 1/8 and 26.38 s at 1/4. The later question was whether just one probe could pay back; it still did not improve choices economically here.'),
('Research repeats are not free online information', 'An actual one-probe decision cannot inspect three repeats and select using their median. Each diagnostic decision receives one observation. Shared preparation is charged to every hypothetical request, rather than amortized across diagnostic repeats to create artificial savings.'),
('The earlier 10.96-second quotation', 'That chat number must not automatically be equated with this slide\'s 10.37 s. The checked artifact reports 10.37 s for the reconstructed aggregate. Without the specific 10.96 s run/receipt, we cannot identify it as total profiling, multiple probes or measured online latency.'),
('What remains unmeasured', 'Metadata/controller wall overhead and the full CPU/RAM exposure of sample preparation are unmeasured. Resource-time is a configured proxy, not utilization or currency. Persistent clusters or sample reuse change the comparison; baselines must receive the same reuse opportunity.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Chhota sample compute jaldi ho sakta hai, par workers start karna aur cleanup phir bhi time leta hai. Sirf sample ka calculation time dekhna total profiling cost nahi hai."'), refs=('R4','R7'))

page(10, 'What 143 tests and 38 tests verify',
('The original-advisor checkpoint', 'Implementation d6f906a passed 143 tests with zero failures, errors or skips in 36.89 s, using the original Windows .venv and Python 3.14.6. pip check passed. The suite included tiny Dask integration fixtures. Evidence was published at caa8a8a. This is not a count of 143 research benchmarks.'),
('The research contract checkpoint', 'The one-probe check at implementation 82d40af passed 38 tests in 0.23 s of test-suite time. It also checked eight default decisions, 24 single-observation counterfactual decisions and 37 termination-record representations using existing evidence. The published checkpoint reference is 7f9c1b0. No new Dask workload or pilot ran.'),
('Why not combine them into an accuracy claim?', 'These are different suites and checkpoints on different paths. They check software requirements and examples. They are not 181 independent scientific experiments, 100% advisor accuracy or proof of arbitrary workload support.'),
('What the 78 fingerprints mean', 'The original receipt identifies 78 source/evidence files. A later report audit clarified a runtime.py docstring. Executable AST was unchanged, but its current byte hash differs. The historical 143-test receipt still documents the original checkpoint, not a new suite run on every later file version.'),
('The presentation distinction', 'Software verification is complete at the recorded checkpoints; fresh experimental validation is a separate next step. Small fixtures and historical replays are not the planned 96-run study. Test-suite seconds should not be mixed into runtime-performance comparisons.'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Test pass ka matlab code checked cases mein sahi behave karta hai. Real workload par recommendation useful hai ya nahi, uske liye fresh measurements chahiye."'), refs=('M7','M12','R8'))

page(10, 'How the prospective 96-run study would work',
('What prospective means', 'Record predictions and selections first, execute the jobs afterward, then compare outcomes. Retrospective replay reuses already observed outcomes. Fresh runs on the original four inputs provide new observations, but do not test generalization to unseen workload profiles.'),
('1. Review and freeze the study', 'After evidence review, obtain a suitable verification receipt for the current software. If source fingerprints no longer match, run verification into a new output directory; do not edit the old receipt. Freeze input hashes, model, settings, deadlines and pre-run advice. This guide describes the workflow without executing preparation.'),
('2. Collect bounded physical attempts', 'Four profiles x eight configurations x three repeats give 96 planned attempts. Use the original host, consistent power/background conditions, a separate study directory and an append-only journal. Preserve every start and outcome. Do not hide failures or selectively retry them to improve the score.'),
('3. Evaluate independently', 'Compare saved predictions with fresh successful runtimes and report error, upper coverage, deadlines and configured resource-time proxies against fixed baselines. Keep failures and unknown values visible. Report acceptance out of four profiles and selected outcomes out of at most twelve attempts separately.'),
('The checked status', 'The software checkpoint passed, while the fresh study is not prepared or started in the checked status records. No fresh validation outputs were found in the checked locations. Update this statement only when an actual later receipt supports it, not because a slide lists it as the next milestone.'),
('Hinglish example', '"Exam ke answers dekhne se pehle apna answer lock karo. Waise hi pehle prediction save hogi, phir job chalegi, phir prediction aur actual compare honge."'), refs=('M1','M7','M12'))

page(11, 'Which claims can you defend?',
('The supported scope', 'The original advisor is bounded to four fixed synthetic profiles and eight declared settings, with results from one Windows host. The 96 original runs contain no failures, so they cannot train a failure-probability model. The current output therefore has failure_probability=null.'),
('What the older 34.1% figure means', 'The older recommend-model policy had a mean median normalized resource-time proxy of 7.524, versus 11.421 for the declared reference. (11.421 - 7.524)/11.421 is approximately 34.1%. It reuses the historical table, including some fitting-source rows. This is not independently demonstrated savings, electricity reduction or cloud billing reduction.'),
('Do not mix the old and new objectives', 'The 34.1% figure belongs to the older policy and objective. The newer balanced advise policy uses a different normalized score. Presenting the old difference as the new policy\'s achieved gain would misattribute the evidence. The slide deliberately calls it an earlier cost comparison.'),
('What the research achieved', 'Quiet samples missed tested full-pressure cases. Worker records revealed that retained periodic peaks can under-observe termination-time memory. Continuous ratios did not improve choices beyond metadata, and single-probe economics were unfavorable. These are useful measurement findings, not a proven universal selector or established novel algorithm.'),
('A strong, accurate conclusion', '"We implemented a constrained advisor and measured a bounded runtime-prediction result. We tested both information and cost in probe research. Probing was not justified for the current short jobs, so it remains disabled. Fresh prospective validation is next."'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Jo measure hua hai wahi claim karo. Purane replay ka 34.1% number naye advisor ki guaranteed saving nahi, aur no failures ka matlab failure risk zero prove hona nahi."'), refs=('M2','M4','M13','R4','R6','R7'))

page(11, 'Next steps: product work and research work',
('Complete the original advisor evaluation', 'Run and evaluate the fresh study under a predeclared protocol. Bind saved advice to execution on the exact verified input. Build a local interface linking recommendations, rejection reasons and actual outcomes. A UI improves usability and integration; it does not itself improve scientific accuracy.'),
('Expand support through a versioned study', 'New sizes, layouts, seeds or hardware need an explicit feature/data contract. Do not bypass the frozen support gate to accept arbitrary workloads. New fitting/calibration and untouched workload-group evaluation are needed. More repeats of a few inputs do not automatically broaden scope.'),
('Require a useful research hypothesis', 'Specify a meaningful case where a probe could change and improve a decision beyond strong metadata. Declare a profiling budget and fair fixed/metadata/analytical baselines. Longer jobs or recurring-job reuse are possible directions, not completed findings. New seeds help, but variants of already inspected recipes alone do not establish broad generalization.'),
('Do not automatically expand a failed gate', 'Old Stage C, the disabled 48-attempt one-probe draft, new training and a main merge are not implied by this guide. Simply collecting more rows does not turn an unsupported method into a contribution. First identify useful extra information and an affordable way to validate it.'),
('A clear closing sentence', '"Next we will validate the original advisor with fresh executions. We will pursue probe research only when there is a specific reason to expect additional decision value beyond its measurement cost."'),
('Hinglish recap', '"Do tracks hain: existing advisor ko properly validate aur integrate karna; aur probe research ko tabhi aage badhana jab extra benefit ka clear reason ho."'), refs=('M1','M7','R7','R9'))

page(12, 'The numbers: a revision map',
('Original advisor', '<b>4</b> fixed profiles; <b>8</b> configurations; <b>32</b> family/configuration groups; <b>96</b> physical runs. <b>36/36/24</b> train/calibration/test rows, corresponding to <b>12/12/8</b> groups. <b>13.84%</b> LightGBM held-out MdAPE; <b>23.01%</b> baseline MdAPE. <b>1.688</b> upper multiplier; <b>24/24</b> observed test-row coverage.'),
('Screen and Stage A', '<b>768</b> screen attempts = 192 full + 576 probes. <b>759/1/8</b> success/timeout/error. <b>48</b> Stage A full attempts, with <b>41</b> successes and <b>7</b> errors. Low-cap successes <b>17/24</b>; high-cap <b>24/24</b>. The completed 48 runs are distinct from the disabled future 48-attempt pilot.'),
('Stage B and workers', '<b>96</b> quiet successful probes. <b>32</b> comparison groups and <b>8</b> pressure discrepancies: four low-cap cases at two fractions. <b>144</b> Stage A+B jobs; <b>321</b> worker lifetimes; <b>37</b> lifetimes with termination events. <b>33/37</b> sampled peaks below threshold; <b>36/37</b> event RSS above sampled peak.'),
('Costs and tests', '<b>4.79 s</b> metadata-associated reconstructed request median; <b>10.37 s</b> always-one-probe median; <b>5.47 s</b> single-probe preparation/lifecycle/preflight median. <b>143</b> main Windows tests; <b>38</b> research tests. <b>0</b> fresh validation attempts in the checked status; <b>96</b> planned.'),
('How to revise', 'For every number, name its study, its unit and its denominator. The same number can recur in unrelated studies. Never merge these counts into one accuracy or reliability score.'),
('Hinglish memory aid', '"Number bolne se pehle socho: kis study ka, kis cheez ka, kitne mein se? 96 original runs, 96 probes aur 96 future runs alag hain."'), refs=('M2','M3','M7','R1','R3','R4','R6','R7','R8'))

page(12, 'Viva practice: short, defensible answers',
('Why use machine learning?', 'We wanted runtime estimates from workload and configuration descriptors. LightGBM was a practical tabular regression option and had lower held-out MdAPE than the family-median baseline. We did not prove it best among every possible model.'),
('How were the probes selected?', 'The screen compared uniform, memory-scaled uniform and partition-tail methods. Stage B focused on memory-scaled uniform at 1/8 and 1/4. The quarter-sample continuous signal had a wider development separation, motivating a one-probe counterfactual. Admission still stayed off because choices did not improve and probing added cost.'),
('Why does a successful sample not establish full safety?', 'Smaller fact input, the cap mapping and the retained full join dimension need not preserve working-set pressure proportionally. We measured quiet samples against pressured full cases. The exact mechanism has not been fully isolated.'),
('Why are experiments pending if tests passed?', 'Tests check software contracts and tiny fixtures. The fresh study collects new runtime and selection outcomes. Correct software and beneficial recommendations are related but different questions.'),
('What is the training accuracy?', 'The quoted 13.84% is held-out MdAPE. A separate training-accuracy number is not reported here. Regression error should not be converted into classification accuracy.'),
('Hinglish answer to "What is your contribution?"', '"Humne constrained advisor, controlled measurements aur sample-profiling ki limitations study ki hain. Universal failure predictor ya guaranteed best algorithm claim nahi kar rahe."'), refs=('M3','M4','M7','R1','R4','R7'))

page(12, 'A connected presentation speaking script',
('Opening: slides 1-3', '"Resource selection involves a trade-off: too little may slow or fail a job, while larger settings increase configured resource burden. SAFE-Pipe recommends deadline-aware configurations for known profiles. A model is fitted offline; requests use saved predictions and explicit checks. The probe research is a separate path."'),
('Original results: slides 4-6', '"We ran four profiles under eight settings three times: 96 executions. Repeats from the same family/configuration stayed together in the split. Held-out MdAPE was 13.84%, versus 23.01% for the baseline. Upper estimates covered 24 test rows within this limited scope. Balanced scoring trades predicted time against configured resources."'),
('Research findings: slides 7-8', '"Stage A had seven errors among 48 full attempts, all at the lower cap. Stage B\'s 96 samples were quiet, yet eight comparison groups missed full pressure. Worker evidence also showed that periodic retained peaks can miss a higher termination-time RSS reading."'),
('Decision and verification: slides 9-10', '"Continuous memory ratios separated cases, but stronger metadata already made the same choices. One-probe replay raised median constructed time from 4.79 to 10.37 seconds. Automatic probing therefore remains disabled. Software checkpoints passed; prospective validation is still pending."'),
('Close: slide 11', '"Results remain bounded to the tested inputs and host. The older 34.1% resource proxy difference is historical replay evidence. Next are fresh validation, exact-input execution binding and a local interface. Probe research needs a specific case for extra information and total-cost benefit."'),
('Hinglish practice tip', '"Pehle English explanation bolo, phir ek chhota example do. Exact mechanism nahi measure hua toh confidently bolo: we have not isolated that yet. Guess karke certainty mat dikhao."'), refs=('M1','M3','M4','R3','R4','R6','R7'))
